ir. So if I have two pairs, that's two pairs, but we need one pair. So maybe it's better to have one pair and form the other melds.\n\nAlternatively, if I can form a Pung or Kong with W5 or W9, that would be good. For example, if I have two W5s and get another W5, that's a Pung. Similarly for W9.\n\nBut the unshown tiles have W5:4, so there are four W5s in total. I have two, so two left. So if I keep the two W5s, there's a chance to get another one.\n\nSimilarly, W9 has four in total, I have two, so two left.\n\nSo, keeping the W5s and W9s is beneficial for potential Pungs.\n\nTherefore, discarding T4 (a single) seems like a good move, as it's not part of any potential meld and freeing up space for more useful tiles.\n\nAnother option is to discard J2 or J3. Let's say I discard J2. Then I have J3 left. If I can get another J3, that's a pair. But there are four J3s in total, and I have one, so three left. So possible. But if I discard J2, maybe another player can claim it for their own meld.\n\nBut compared to discarding T4, which is a single and not part of any meld, I think discarding T4 is better.\n\nSo, the best move is to play T4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The optimal move is to discard T4 as it is a single tile with low utility for forming melds, allowing more flexibility to develop higher-value combinations like pairs or pungs with W5, W9, or honor tiles.  \nReason: Discarding T4 removes a non-contributory tile, preserving critical pairs (W5x2, W9x2) and potential honor melds (J2/J3). This maximizes chances to complete the hand with additional W5/W9 pungs or honor pairs while avoiding blocking key tiles for future draws.  \nAnswer: Play T4"}
