ed to form four melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\n- T6-T7-T8 (Chow)\n- W5-W6-W7 (Chow)\n- W9-W9 (pair)\n- J2-J2 (pair)\n\nWait, that's four melds and a pair? No, four melds would be four groups of three. So two Chows (each 3) and two Pungs (each 3). But I don't have two Pungs. Wait, no. The four melds would be two Chows and two Pungs. But I have two Chows and two pairs. That's not valid. So this is not a winning hand. So I'm missing something here.\n\nAh, right. The four melds must be three of a kind (Pung, Kong) or sequences (Chow). So if I have two Chows and two Pungs, that's four melds. But in my case, I have two Chows and two pairs. That's not valid. So I need to adjust.\n\nMaybe I need to form one more Pung. For example, if I have two J2s and can form a Pung, but I need one more. So if I can draw a J2, then I have a Pung. But until then, it's not possible. So the current hand isn't a winning hand yet. So I need to discard a tile that allows me to eventually form a winning hand.\n\nGiven that, discarding J3 seems safe. It doesn't help others much, and keeps my other tiles intact. So the best move is to discard J3.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential to form two Chows (T6-T7-T8 and W5-W6-W7) and pairs in J2 and W9. Discarding J3 (a single honor tile with no immediate meld potential) minimizes risk to others and preserves key tiles for future melds.]\nReason: [Discarding J3 avoids exposing a tile that could help opponents form Pungs while retaining flexibility to complete additional Chows or pairs. The hand's structure suggests waiting for a third J2 or a W5/W6/W7 completion.]\nAnswer: Play J3"}
